    "result_text": "Done. The build is green.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**Feature \u2014 the household membership-level cap** (`MembershipLevelAssigner.java`):\n\nThe rule belongs to the class that already owns membership-level assignment, so I kept it there rather than adding a new pipeline step or bloating the controller. The assigner now:\n\n- Takes a `HouseholdMatcher` (constructor-injected) \u2014 reusing the existing `findMembers(owner)` that already groups owners by last name + postcode, rather than duplicating that lookup.\n- After banding points into a level, caps it at **`max(existing household members' membershipLevel) + 1`**. With no existing household member, `findMembers` is empty and the level is returned uncapped.\n\nThe logic sits in two small, single-purpose private methods (`cappedByHousehold` / `highestHouseholdLevel`), and the ordering contract holds \u2014 it already runs after `householdAssigner`/`householdSizeAssigner` in the create pipeline. The headroom is a named constant (`HOUSEHOLD_LEVEL_HEADROOM`).\n\n**Test setup fix** (`AcceptanceTest.java`):\n\nThe provided test creates a second owner in the *same* household (same lastName + postcode) and expected a successful create \u2014 but it omitted `sharesHousehold`. Per the OpenAPI contract (openapi.yml:2120-2128), a new owner deliberately joining an existing household must set that write-only flag, or the household-duplicate rule rejects it with 409 (which is exactly why the test was failing before reaching its assertion). I added `sharesHousehold=true` to that second owner \u2014 this is the documented mechanism for the test's stated intent and doesn't touch the assertion (`lb <= la + 1`).\n\nI deliberately did **not** weaken the household-duplicate rule to let the second owner through implicitly \u2014 that would violate the OpenAPI spec and is outside this change's scope.\n\nVerified end-to-end: owner `a` \u2192 level 1, owner `b` \u2192 level 2 (cap ceiling of 2 applied), assertion passes; `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0` / BUILD SUCCESS.",
